  *Abstract.* Architecture models describe what a system's components may do; they do not constrain it. This paper reports a demonstrator in which the model constrains the artifact _mechanically_: the primary artifact is a _signal graph_ — a functional reactive program in which each node declares its authority as typed capability handles in a `with` clause — and a node's `with` clause determines the set of interfaces its compiled WebAssembly component is permitted to import, derived from the graph and checked against the built binary, so a world that over-grants fails the build rather than shipping. The distinction this buys is one we found only by building the tier twice: an earlier WASI-context build confined nodes by the _host's configuration_ while its modules still imported `fd_write` and `path_open`, whereas components built with no WASI adapter import no filesystem, socket, environment, or clock function beyond what a `with` clause deliberately grants — and a node granted a clock imports exactly the one WASI interface its clause names. Confinement becomes a property of the artifact rather than of how a host happened to configure it, and only the latter survives a misconfigured deployment.

  Alongside it we report a graph validator implementing six classes of static analysis, of which the load-bearing one is a trust lattice with no upward coercion, applied uniformly to edges and node bodies: trust laundering — which type-checks on _every edge_ once the consumer's declared input is widened — is caught as a lattice violation rather than a type mismatch, and an evaluation harness pins each rejection to the analysis that must produce it, so the distinction cannot silently rot. A capability-boundary crossing costs #d.crossing_us µs even though every crossing marshals typed values. We are explicit about what does not follow. The mutation corpus is curated and illustrative, so its counts are not a soundness result and no noninterference proof is attempted; the host tier's escapes are a real gap, recorded as passing tests rather than omitted; and prompt injection is _attenuated, not eliminated_ — adversarial text survives in a permitted free-text field even on the confined tier, and what bounds the damage is the capability scope rather than the type or the sandbox. Against runtime defences that enforce capability policies inside an agent's execution @debenedetti_defeating_2025, the position here is complementary and narrower: enforcement is static, before anything runs, across a composed system, with the component's import table as the backstop.
]

