= Introduction <sec:intro>

For thirty years, a small community of researchers pursued an idea that working software developers consistently rejected: that source code should be a structured graph, not text in files. Projectional editors @jetbrains_mps_2025, structure editors @simonyi_intentional_2006, model-driven architecture @omg_mda_2014: the history of these efforts is a history of elegant ideas that foundered on a single obstacle: programmers prefer text editors, value syntactic flexibility for half-formed thoughts, and resist representations that constrain expression before intent is clear.

That obstacle has moved. The rapid adoption of AI coding agents (Claude Code @anthropic_claude_code_2025, Codex @openai_codex_2025, Cursor @anysphere_cursor_2025, Windsurf @codeium_windsurf_2025) has introduced a new primary author of implementation code that does not _require_ the specific affordances of text editing that motivated human resistance to structured representations. What agents benefit from instead is semantically rich, machine-readable representations of intent that prevent entire categories of error before generation begins. Recent work on LLM code generation within the Hazel typed-hole environment @blinn_statically_2024 gives direct empirical evidence: providing agents with rich static context from the type system substantially improves generation quality. The graph representation that human developers rejected may be precisely what agent-authored software requires — and the parallel emergence of spec-driven development frameworks (OpenSpec @fission_openspec_2025, GitHub's Spec Kit @github_spec_kit_2025, AWS's Kiro @aws_kiro_2025) is evidence that practitioners have already begun treating structured intent, rather than generated implementation, as the artifact that matters.

Earlier work @lavi_architecture_2026 argued that these trends converge on a development model built around a _signal graph_: a program written in a functional reactive style in which each node is a function from typed inputs to typed outputs, all authority is held as explicitly declared capability handles, and trust is a propagating type-level annotation. In that model the graph is simultaneously the architecture model, the security policy, and the program; implementation code inside nodes is a compiled artifact. That paper was written before any of it had been built, and said so: its security claims were stated conditionally throughout — properties that _would_ hold in a sound realisation of a type system that did not yet exist.

This paper reports what happens when that model is built, and defends one property of the result that the vision did not predict and that we found only by building it: that the model can determine the artifact's authority _mechanically_, so that a component's confinement is checkable against the graph rather than asserted alongside it.

== The central claim <sec:claim>

The claim this paper defends is deliberately narrower than the vision's, and is chosen to be falsifiable by the artifact accompanying it:

#block(width: 100%, inset: (x: 1.5em, y: 0.6em), stroke: (left: 2pt + luma(140)))[
  A node's declared capabilities can _determine_ the authority surface of its
  compiled artifact: the set of interfaces its WebAssembly component may import
  is derived from its `with` clause in the architecture model and checked
  against the built binary, so confinement is a property of the artifact rather
  than of the host's configuration and an over-granting world fails the build.
  On the same model, a trust lattice with no upward coercion, applied uniformly
  to edges and node bodies, _rejects at assembly time_ — before any node
  executes — wirings that conventional architectures can only discourage by
  review, including the trust-laundering case that type-checks on every edge.
  Both hold at a capability-boundary cost of tens of microseconds.
]

What this claim does not say is as important as what it does. It does not say the system is provably secure: no soundness argument connects well-typed wiring to noninterference here (@sec:threat-security). It does not say prompt injection is prevented: it is attenuated, and @sec:eval-injection reports the residual that survives on the confined tier. It does not say the corpus of caught mistakes is complete: it is curated, and counts of caught mutations are evidence of implementability, not of coverage (@sec:threats). And it is a claim about _confinement_, not about the correctness of what a confined node computes. A reader who finishes this paper believing a stronger claim has been shown has been misled by it, and we have tried throughout to make that outcome difficult.

== Contributions <sec:contributions>

- *A capability surface derived from the architecture model and checked against the artifact*: each node's permitted import set is computed from its `with` clause in the canonical graph and compared against what its built WASM component actually imports, so an over-granting world fails rather than ships — and, because the components carry no WASI adapter, the absence of filesystem, socket, environment, and clock authority is structural rather than configured (@sec:sandbox-tier).
- *A graph validator* implementing six classes of analysis over canonical graph definitions — edge type-compatibility, a flow-sensitive trust lattice, variant completeness, capability narrowing at composition, intra-graph consistency, and cross-graph signature matching — in which trust laundering is a violation of the _same_ no-upward-coercion order that governs edges, rather than a rule bolted on beside edge typing (@sec:validator).
- *An executable runtime with two enforcement tiers* that composes in one graph: host-level object discipline, whose escapes are recorded as passing tests rather than omitted, and the confined tier above (@sec:tiers).
- *Operational hierarchical composition*: a node whose name resolves to another graph executes by nested assembly, from which capability confinement across the composition boundary follows from the plumbing rather than from a rule (@sec:subgraph).
- *Capability identity, revocation, and rotation* expressed in the graph source — identity and revocation enforced on both tiers, rotation on the host tier — resolving a prerequisite the vision did not notice its own revocation problem had (@sec:identity).
- *A contract layer sized to stay checkable*: pre- and postconditions over a closed predicate vocabulary, with Racket-style blame separating a failed precondition from a failed postcondition, and a specification outside the vocabulary rejected before anything runs rather than quietly evaluating to false (@sec:contracts).
- *An evaluation* — the graph-to-binary derivation reported per node, corpus verdicts pinned to the reason class that must catch them, boundary overhead against the vision's stated envelope, and prompt-injection outcomes on both tiers — emitted as a reproducible artifact so that every figure in @sec:evaluation traces to a single run, and a regression is caught rather than silently absorbed.

== Relation to the rest of the corpus <sec:relation>

The earlier paper @lavi_architecture_2026 set out this design and its security claims before any of it had been built, and hedged them accordingly; it is published and frozen. This paper does not revise those predictions to match what was found, and it does not audit them either: the prediction-by-prediction accounting, together with the protocol under which the vision was frozen before building, is the subject of the companion paper @lavi_predicting_2026. Keeping the accounting in one document rather than two is deliberate — two accountings that can disagree is exactly the drift this corpus is built to prevent. What this paper owes the reader is narrower and is discharged in @sec:established: a plain statement of what its own evidence does and does not support.

The remainder of the paper is organised as follows. @sec:design presents the design the demonstrator implements, restated from the earlier paper so this one stands alone. @sec:implementation reports what was built. @sec:evaluation evaluates it and @sec:established says what that evaluation establishes. @sec:related positions the work against prior art, and @sec:threats states the limits of what has been shown together with the open problems that bound it.

= Design <sec:design>

This section presents the design the demonstrator implements. It develops the design set out in the earlier paper @lavi_architecture_2026, restated here so that this paper stands alone and cut to what @sec:implementation and @sec:evaluation depend on; readers of that paper may skim to @sec:implementation. One convention is used throughout and is load-bearing: claims the demonstrator has since substantiated are stated in the present tense, and claims it has not are left in the conditional mood the vision wrote them in. This section describes intent as much as artifact, so a conditional here is deliberate rather than stylistic — @sec:established says which is which.

The model has four interlocking properties.

*The signal graph as source of truth.* The primary artifact that humans author, review, and version-control is a functional reactive program (FRP — see @sec:frp for the paradigm and its history): a directed graph in which each node is a function from time-varying typed inputs to time-varying typed outputs, with any effects mediated through explicitly declared capability handles. This graph is simultaneously the architecture model, the security policy, and the program. It _is_ the implementation at the level of abstraction humans author and review; the code inside each node is intended to be a generated artifact derived from it — the one property of the model this demonstrator does not exercise, and the reason its central workflow claim remains open (@sec:conclusion).

*Capabilities as declared requirements.* Nodes have no ambient authority: side effects (database access, network calls, LLM invocations, event emission) are not capabilities that code can reach for from the surrounding environment. They are typed capability handles declared separately from data inputs using a `with` clause — for example, a node with signature `(OrderRequest) → OrderConfirmation with DBHandle<'orders', read-write>, EventEmitter<'order-events'>` can read and write orders and emit order events, and nothing else, because no other mechanism exists within its scope. A node with no `with` clause is a pure function of its inputs in the conventional sense. Whether that absence of ambient authority is an _enforced_ property or only an object discipline a hostile body can reach around is a fact about the tier a node runs on, not about the model — a distinction the vision did not draw and @sec:tiers and @sec:eval-tiers report on both sides of.

*Code as compiled artifact.* AI agents would generate the implementation of each node to satisfy the behavioural contracts encoded in the graph's type signatures — the one part of the model this demonstrator does not exercise, since every node body in it was written by hand (@sec:conclusion). The imperative code inside each node is an implementation detail, analogous to compiled bytecode. It can be regenerated, refactored, or rewritten without changing the system's meaning, provided it satisfies its contracts — an interchangeability claim that holds up to the expressiveness of those contracts, a limit examined in @sec:open. Humans review graph transformations; they do not routinely review generated code.

*Security by construction.* Because capabilities are injected and the type system propagates trust annotations, security properties are intended to be structural invariants of the graph rather than aspirations enforced by code inspection. The strength of the resulting guarantee varies sharply by vulnerability class — from statically ill-typed, through prevented-by-construction, to merely attenuated — and @sec:security names each in turn, because a model whose guarantees are described uniformly will be read as stronger than it is.

== Functional reactive programming as the conceptual core <sec:frp>

The signal graph model is not a novel metaphor. It is a direct application of _functional reactive programming_ (FRP), a paradigm with a nearly thirty-year research history, here elevated from a UI programming technique to a whole-system architectural substrate.

=== FRP: a brief account <sec:frp-brief>

FRP was introduced by Elliott and Hudak's 1997 _Functional Reactive Animation_ @elliott_functional_1997, which modelled interactive behaviour as pure functions over continuous time, composed without state mutation or callbacks; Wan and Hudak @wan_functional_2000 gave it a rigorous stream-based semantics, and, building on Hughes's arrows @hughes_generalising_2000, Nilsson, Courtney, and Peterson developed _arrowized FRP_ @nilsson_functional_2002, which makes _signal functions_ the composable unit and their interface explicit in the type: a signal function `SF a b` transforms a signal of `a` values into a signal of `b` values. That is the formal object this work extends with capability annotation. The paradigm has been realised widely — Elm @czaplicki_elm_2012, RxJS @rxjs_2025, and Cycle.js @cyclejs_2025, the last with a driver discipline close to capability injection, lacking only fine-grained capability typing and trust propagation — alongside a parallel graph-based tradition in the synchronous dataflow languages (Lustre @halbwachs_synchronous_1991, Esterel @berry_esterel_1992, Signal @benveniste_signal_1991) and flow-based programming @morrison_flow-based_1994, and at commercial scale in LabVIEW @ni_labview_2025 and Simulink @mathworks_simulink_2025, which also illustrate the comprehension problems graph representations bring (@sec:threat-external). Differential computation @mcsherry_differential_2013 @budiu_dbsp_2023 is the line on which any claim that such a graph could serve as a production substrate would depend; the demonstrator implements none of it (@sec:threat-external).

*A concrete illustration.* To motivate the extensions developed in @sec:frp-architecture, consider a node that processes user-submitted text and passes it to an LLM with tool-calling access. In a conventional system, this is dangerous: if the submitted text contains adversarial instructions, the LLM may execute them using its tools. The vulnerability is not a bug in any individual component; it is an architectural property: the unintended flow from untrusted input to a privileged executor — the pattern that @greshake_not_2023 documented as indirect prompt injection against deployed LLM applications.

In the signal graph, the same scenario is expressed as two nodes. The first, `UserInputHandler`, has signature:

```
UserInputHandler : (HTTPRequest<'POST', 'user:message'>) → Untrusted<UserMessage>
```

The second, `LLMOrchestrator`, has signature:

```
LLMOrchestrator : (SanitisedPrompt) → AgentResponse
  with LLMClient<[respond, lookup]>
```

A direct wiring from `UserInputHandler`'s output to `LLMOrchestrator`'s input is a _type error_: `Untrusted<UserMessage>` does not match `SanitisedPrompt`. The graph cannot be assembled without an explicit node that transforms `Untrusted<UserMessage>` into `SanitisedPrompt` — a node whose existence is visible in the architecture, whose implementation is subject to contract verification, and whose presence is required by the type system rather than by a policy document. In a well-typed realisation of this model, the prompt injection vulnerability would be _ill-typed_: no well-typed graph could express it. The type system design that would deliver this guarantee remains undesigned (@sec:open); the example illustrates the target property, not a proven result.

#figure(
  image("/dist/diagrams/typed-wiring.svg", width: 80%),
  caption: [The direct wiring of the two nodes above is structurally rejected: the output type `Untrusted<UserMessage>` does not inhabit the input type `SanitisedPrompt`. An explicit sanitisation node — not shown — would be required by the type system to close the gap. Security, here, would be a property of graph shape.],
) <fig:typed-wiring>

=== Extending FRP: capability annotation and trust tainting <sec:frp-architecture>

The step from FRP as a UI technique to FRP as a whole-system architectural model requires two extensions that the existing literature does not fully address.

The first is _capability annotation_. Standard FRP treats effects as values managed by the runtime, but does not give them a fine-grained type structure that distinguishes, say, a read-only database handle from a read-write one, or a sanitised string from an untrusted one. The object-capability model @miller_robust_2006 provides the missing ingredient: capabilities are unforgeable typed references whose possession is the proof of authorisation. Combining FRP's signal graph semantics with the object-capability model's typed authority gives us signal graphs in which data-flow and capability-flow are both first-class, typed, and statically checkable.

The second is _trust tainting_. Data entering the graph from untrusted sources (user input, third-party API responses, LLM outputs) carries a type marker that propagates through signal transformations until it passes through an explicitly designated sanitisation node. This is analogous to taint tracking as studied in information-flow security @sabelfeld_language-based_2003, and specifically to the labelled-IO approach demonstrated by practical information-flow control libraries such as LIO @stefan_flexible_2011, but expressed as ordinary type-level propagation rather than a separate analysis. A node emitting `Untrusted<string>` could not then be wired into a node holding `LLMClient<[respond, lookup]>`; the type system would reject the edge. The graph topology would enforce the security property without separate analysis.

== The signal graph <sec:signal-graph>

The primary artifact is a version-controlled, typed signal graph with the following structure.

*Nodes* are functions with explicit signatures. A node's signature has two parts: its _data inputs_ (typed signals from upstream nodes) and its _capability requirements_ (typed handles to external resources, declared with a `with` clause). The data inputs describe what the node transforms; the capability requirements describe what authority it has. This separation reflects a lifecycle distinction: data signals flow at runtime as the graph propagates, while capabilities are provisioned when the graph is instantiated. A node with no `with` clause is a pure function of its inputs; a node with a `with` clause is pure with respect to its inputs _given_ its handles, with all effects mediated through those handles.

_A note on the design choice._ An alternative treats capabilities as ordinary typed parameters, consistent with the object-capability principle that capabilities are just values; we separate them syntactically because "what a node transforms" and "what authority it holds" serve different review concerns, and because capabilities bind at construction while data flows at invocation.

*Edges* are typed data connections between nodes. An edge from node A's output to node B's input is valid only if the types match. Edges carry data; capabilities are not wired through edges but provisioned via `with` clauses. This is a deliberate restriction. Object-capability systems in the E lineage permit dynamic delegation by passing capability references through messages, which is flexible but defeats static analysis of the authority topology. The signal graph trades that flexibility for a fully statically analysable capability distribution; the distributed extension of this trade-off is a separate problem, and belongs to the corpus's forward agenda @lavi_predicting_2026 rather than to the limits of this result. The `with` clauses collectively constitute the architecture's security policy, expressed as reviewable graph structure rather than prose. Because the graph's parameter list declares all external dependencies, swapping a production capability for a mock (replacing a live `DBHandle` with a test fixture, for example) requires only a change at the graph boundary — no node signature changes, since the `with` clause names a type, not a specific instance.

*Trust annotations* (the type-level markers introduced as trust tainting in @sec:frp) propagate through the graph. Data entering from untrusted sources carries a type marker, `Untrusted<T>`, that is preserved through transformations until explicitly discharged. In a well-typed realisation, the type system would prevent `Untrusted<T>` from reaching a node that accepts only `T`. Discharge is most effective when it is not merely a label removal but a _type transformation_: converting unstructured input into a constrained representation whose structure limits what downstream nodes can receive. The combination of trust propagation and structural typing is what would deliver the security properties claimed in @sec:security.

An open design question must be acknowledged here: local node typing is necessary but not sufficient. The full guarantee requires the _wiring_ to be checked too — an untrusted source must not reach a node expecting a clean `T` through a widening coercion — which is the standard coercion problem in information-flow type systems #cite(<sabelfeld_language-based_2003>, supplement: [§3]): the subtyping relation between `Untrusted<T>` and `T` must be absent, or equivalently, wiring checks must be flow-sensitive in trust. The literature offers well-understood tools — security labels in the style of Jif @myers_decentralized_1997, or a lattice with no upward coercion — and the demonstrator implements a first realisation of the latter (@sec:validator). What remains open is the choice among two-point, graded (`Untrusted ⊑ Sanitised ⊑ Trusted`), and decentralised-label designs, and, critically, a soundness argument that well-typed wiring implies noninterference, which nothing here attempts (@sec:open). The claim is not that the problem is solved but that it is tractable and belongs in the type system.

*Behavioural contracts* are attached to node signatures as pre- and postconditions. These are the specifications against which AI-generated implementations would be verified, and the stable interface across which different implementations are interchangeable. The demonstrator implements a deliberately small instance of this — a closed predicate vocabulary, checked at the executor boundary with blame (@sec:contracts) — but the language the model ultimately wants is undesigned (@sec:open): candidates range from refinement types in the Liquid Haskell tradition, through Dafny- or Ada-style declarative specifications, to lightweight examples-and-invariants in the QuickCheck tradition. Contracts are authored alongside node signatures — either by the developer at graph-review time, or proposed by the agent during intent capture and confirmed on review — and would be checked by a combination of static analysis (for properties the type system expresses directly) and property-based or contract-based testing at implementation time (for properties that require runtime evidence). The tractability trade-off between contract expressiveness and automatic verification is addressed in @sec:open.

== A concrete graph <sec:concrete-graph>

The following pseudocode sketches an AI customer support agent as a signal graph. The domain was chosen because the properties under test are all visible in it: untrusted user input, LLM invocations with and without tool access, and fine-grained capability distinctions. No concrete syntax has been designed; the value here is the structural shape, not the notation.

_Syntax conventions._ Types are angle-bracketed: `T<...>`. Capability types are parameterised by scope, so `DBHandle<'knowledge-base', read>` is a handle to that database in `read` mode, where `read`, `append`, and `read-write` are modes in a permission lattice (a wider mode may be supplied where a narrower is required; `read` and `append` are mutually incomparable). `LLMClient<inference>` grants model access without tool-calling; `LLMClient<[lookup]>` grants one named tool. Sum types carry role labels — `ok: ModeratedQuery | violation: PolicyViolation | escalation: EscalationRequest` — and an edge addresses a variant as `Node.role`.

#raw(read("/dist/graphs/customer-support.graph"), block: true)

The parameter list is the system's authority manifest: an inbound request, a database handle, two LLM clients at different permission levels, a response channel, and an event emitter. In production these bind to real infrastructure; in testing, to mocks or fixtures — a swap at the graph boundary that changes no node signature, since a `with` clause names a type, not an instance. Because the graph itself has a typed signature, it can serve as a node in a larger graph, and `SupportPlatform` (@fig:support-platform) does exactly that: `CustomerSupport` appears there as a single node beside `AgentDashboard` and `BillingService`, its #ev.graphs.customer_support_nodes nodes and their trust zones encapsulated, with the reviewer seeing only what authority each service holds. That `BillingService` has read-write billing access and no LLM capability at all is legible at that altitude without opening it.

#figure(
  image("/dist/graphs/support-platform.svg", width: 75%),
  caption: [The SupportPlatform composition graph. Each service node is a sub-graph (the #(ev.graphs.customer_support_nodes)-node CustomerSupport graph is now a single node). Capability annotations show the authority distribution at the platform level; where a service holds a distinct named instance of a capability type, the identity label is shown after `@` (the two services holding `ResponseChannel<user-session>` are routed distinct `customer_session` and `billing_session` instances). The audit node collects outcomes from all services with append-only database access.],
) <fig:support-platform>

Composition raises two questions this work does not claim to have settled. At the output side, the boundary type is spelled as the union of `CustomerSupport`'s terminal types — `DeliveryConfirmation | EscalationTicket`, written out because the graph language has no alias mechanism (@sec:subgraph) — which is the simplest of several designs for collapsing several terminals into the single boundary type a parent declares. At the input side, when the platform provisions `LLMClient<inference>` to `CustomerSupport`, the sub-graph must route it to the nodes that require it and no others; how a sub-graph exposes internal capability requirements at its boundary is open (@sec:open). The narrower question of which _instance_ a parent routes is settled and expressible in the graph source: `CustomerSupport` and `BillingService` both require `ResponseChannel<user-session>`, and the platform binds each a distinct named instance, so identity crosses the composition boundary rather than being aliased by type (@sec:identity, @sec:subgraph).

#figure(
  image("/dist/graphs/customer-support.svg", width: 85%),
  caption: [The CustomerSupport signal graph. Red shading marks the untrusted zone; green shading marks the structured region. Edges show data flow; capability requirements are annotated on each node. LLM access (blue) is graduated: inference-only for parsing and moderation, a single lookup tool for response generation.],
) <fig:customer-support>

Three properties are visible in that diagram without reading implementation code, and they are what @sec:evaluation puts under test.

_Prompt injection is addressed through structural typing and topological constraint._ `ParseMessage` does not merely strip the `Untrusted` wrapper: it transforms unstructured input into a constrained representation — a classified intent from a finite set, extracted entity references, bounded text fields — and the raw message is consumed, so downstream nodes never see it. `ModerateContent` refines that into a distinct `ModeratedQuery`, recording at the type level that moderation occurred, so a wiring that bypasses moderation is ill-typed and the step is load-bearing rather than advisory. The trust placement this entails is worth naming: the LLM is itself the discharging component, bounded by its capability shape (`LLMClient<inference>` grants no tool use) and by the schema of what it emits. The tool-capable `GenerateResponse` receives only `ConversationContext` assembled downstream of moderation — never raw user text — so no direct path from untrusted input to a tool-capable LLM exists in the graph; in a sound realisation it would be ill-typed. Two limits attach. Capability restriction stops the LLM _acting_ on adversarial instructions but not being _influenced_ in its classification, so a crafted message can cause a misroute; the attack surface narrows from arbitrary tool execution to incorrect routing within a typed pipeline, and bounding that further depends on user-scoped capabilities, which are undesigned (@sec:open). And the schema guarantee holds only _provided the schema excludes unbounded free text in positions flowing to privileged nodes_ — most schemas keep such a field, for the original question itself, and @sec:eval-injection measures exactly that residual.

_Capability distribution is minimal and visible._ The `with` clauses are a complete manifest. `ReceiveMessage` is pure; `ParseMessage` and `ModerateContent` hold inference-only LLM access; `FetchContext` read-only knowledge-base access; `GenerateResponse` a single lookup tool plus that handle; the terminal nodes only a session-scoped response channel; `EscalateToHuman` may emit to the support queue but touches no database. No node has more authority than its function requires, and a new requirement appears in a diff as a new `with` clause.

_Conditional routing and error handling are explicit._ `ModerateContent` produces a three-way union routed to the response pipeline, to user notification, and to human escalation; `GenerateResponse` returns `AgentResponse | LLMError`. The validator enforces that every declared variant has a consumer, but cannot force an implementation to emit the _correct_ variant — a `ModerateContent` that always emits `.ok` is well-typed and behaviourally wrong. That obligation sits outside the closed contract vocabulary of @sec:contracts, which can constrain the shape of an emitted variant but cannot relate the choice among variants to a judgement about the input (@sec:open), as is the routing of transient infrastructure failures the example elides.


== Security properties <sec:security>

The capability-injection model is intended to provide guarantees qualitatively different from those achievable by code review or runtime monitoring. Their strength varies by vulnerability class, and the variation is the point.

*Injection attacks.* SQL and command injection depend on untrusted input reaching an interpreter in executable form. A SQL-executing capability would accept typed queries, not raw strings, so `Untrusted<string>` could not reach it without transiting a sanitisation node that produces a typed query. In a sound realisation the pattern would be ill-typed.

*Privilege escalation.* A node would not acquire capabilities it was not given, the graph being the complete and sole description of the capability distribution, and would extend to the memory level on CHERI hardware — on which nothing here has run (@sec:cheri). Three things bound this, and the order is deliberate, because the last is the one that most changes how the paragraph should be read. Two are caveats about the design: a node holding a network capability can in principle acquire authority out-of-band from services the graph does not model, a distributed-authority problem this work inherits rather than addresses @lavi_predicting_2026; and for user-scoped operations the guarantee is only as strong as an undesigned principal binding (@sec:open). The third is not a caveat but a measured fact about the artifact — the property holds on the confined tier and only as an object discipline on the host tier, where an ungranted capability is reachable and the escape is recorded as a passing test (@sec:eval-tiers).

*Supply chain attacks.* The defence is _capability scoping_, not I/O denial: a malicious library inherits only the scope of the node it inhabits — one database in one mode, an LLM client restricted to a tool allowlist — so the blast radius is that scope rather than the library's creativity. This holds as a property of the artifact on the confined tier and only as an object discipline a hostile library can reach around on the host tier (@sec:eval-tiers). A new capability requirement surfaces in the graph diff as a new `with` clause: a reviewable change, not an implicit elevation.

*Prompt injection and confused deputies.* Structurally attenuated, not eliminated. No well-typed wiring would connect an untrusted source to a tool-capable LLM without transiting a type that constrains the downstream payload, but a free-text field reaching an LLM-capable node stays adversarial even when its wrapper is non-`Untrusted`; strength depends on schema discipline, and @sec:eval-injection measures the residual. The confused-deputy case adds a second mechanism — capabilities bound at the graph boundary to the calling user's credentials, so downstream nodes operate only within that user's authorised scope — whose design is the open item of @sec:open.

*Covert channels remain an open concern.* A node granted a permitted capability can encode information into the timing, query shape, or ordering of its legitimate outputs — channels the type system does not model, and which general covert-channel elimination, a known-hard problem, is not claimed to close. @sec:threat-security states the limit; characterising which channels the model closes, narrows, and leaves open belongs to the forward agenda @lavi_predicting_2026.

= Implementation <sec:implementation>

The demonstrator is a working, tested implementation of the graph-level analyses and the capability-injected runtime described in @sec:design. It is deliberately small, and its scope should be stated before its results are: it is *not* the language the vision called for. It implements no noninterference proof, no dependent types, and no visual editor — the inspector reported in @sec:trace views and runs graphs, but cannot author them — and its contract layer checks rather than verifies (@sec:contracts). What it does implement is the part of the vision whose implementability was least obvious from the outside — that a graph carrying capability and trust annotations can be statically analysed to reject unsafe wirings, and that node bodies can be confined to exactly the authority their signatures declare — and it does so with enough tooling to be run, tested, and measured.

Everything in this section is built and tested; the present tense is used accordingly. Where a property is asserted by a test, that is said: a claim backed by a test cannot silently drift from the artifact the way prose can.

== Canonical graphs and the type grammar <sec:impl-graphs>

The graph definitions in `graphs/*.json` are the single source of truth, validated against a JSON Schema. Both the pseudocode listings and the SVG diagrams reproduced in @sec:concrete-graph are generated from them, so the figures in this paper cannot drift from the graphs the validator and runtime actually consume — the two graphs shown are the two graphs run.

A small type-expression parser reads the capability-annotated type grammar: angle-bracketed generics, capability scopes and modes, and sum types with role labels. The documented grammar is itself emitted from the parser as a build artifact rather than maintained by hand, on the same principle.

== The validator: six graph-level analyses <sec:validator>

A dependency-free validator implements six classes of analysis over the canonical graphs.

/ Edge type-compatibility: source output types, with sum-type variant resolution, must match target input types in data shape, independently of trust.
/ Trust-lattice checking: trust levels form a two-point lattice `Untrusted ⊑ Trusted` with no upward coercion — `Untrusted<T>` is not a subtype of `T` — and the wiring check is flow-sensitive with respect to trust, applied uniformly to edges *and* to node bodies. A node may raise trust only if it is declared a discharger with `discharges_trust: true`, as `ParseMessage` is in the `CustomerSupport` graph.
/ Variant completeness: every declared sum-type output variant must have at least one consuming edge, so dead branches are visible at the graph level rather than hidden inside node implementations.
/ Capability narrowing: at cross-graph composition a parent may provide a handle whose authority exceeds what the sub-graph declares — a wider `LLMClient<[...]>` tool set, a `DBHandle<X, read-write>` where `read` is required, or an `HTTPClient<[...]>` host allowlist that is a superset of the child's (the first scope that is a _set_, narrowing by inclusion) — while strict equality still governs every data-flow position.
/ Intra-graph consistency: every declared capability is used, every boundary data input is consumed, and every edge references a valid node and output variant.
/ Cross-graph signature matching: a node whose name matches another graph's name must satisfy that graph's parameter list position by position.

The second of these is the one worth dwelling on, because its design changed under implementation and the change is a result rather than a detail. Trust is *not* a rule beside edge typing; it is the same no-upward-coercion order, applied to two places. The consequence is what @sec:eval-corpus measures: the blunt unsafe wiring — untrusted input run straight into the tool-capable node — fails as an ordinary data-shape mismatch, but the subtle one survives that check. Widen the tool-capable node's declared input to `Untrusted<RawMessage>` and every edge in the graph type-checks on data shape. The graph is still rejected, because the tool-capable node now emits a non-`Untrusted` output from an `Untrusted<_>` input without being a declared discharger, which is an upward coercion under the lattice.

That case is the argument for the discipline. An earlier design that folded trust into ordinary subtyping — admitting `Untrusted<T> <: T` — would have accepted the widened graph, with the failure invisible at the level of edge types. Trust laundering is thereby one violation of one principled order, and local edge data-compatibility is shown not to be the property worth checking. What the validator does *not* establish is soundness: that every graph well-typed under this lattice satisfies noninterference. That obligation is untouched (@sec:open).

== Two enforcement tiers <sec:tiers>

The runtime loads the same canonical graph JSON, instantiates each node with injected capability handles, and propagates signals along the active path. Nodes run on one of two enforcement tiers, and the runtime reports which ran each node. The distinction between them is the whole point, so it is stated precisely.

=== The host tier <sec:host-tier>

The default tier is *host-level object discipline*, not unforgeable containment. Each node receives only the handles its signature declares, and each handle exposes only the operations its type permits: a handle for `LLMClient<inference>` has no tool-calling operation at all, and one for `LLMClient<[lookup]>` refuses every other tool. This demonstrates the _shape_ of the guarantee. It does not enforce it against a node that declines to play by the object model — nothing stops a hostile Python node from importing `os` and reading the filesystem directly.

That gap is not left as a caveat in prose. A hostile-node suite asserts that filesystem, network, environment, and ungranted-capability escapes *succeed* on this tier. The gap is recorded as a passing test, which is the only form in which it cannot be forgotten, and it is reported in @sec:eval-tiers rather than omitted from the evaluation.

=== The confined tier <sec:sandbox-tier>

The second tier closes that gap for the nodes ported to it. Node bodies are compiled to WebAssembly *components* (@sec:sandboxing) — Rust, via `wit-bindgen`, to `wasm32-unknown-unknown`, converted to a component with `wasm-tools` — and run under `wasmtime`. Each capability kind is declared as a typed WIT interface: `LLMClient<inference>` becomes an interface offering a single inference function, `DBHandle<'knowledge-base', read>` one offering `lookup` and no writer. A node's `with` clause is then precisely the set of interfaces its component imports.

On this tier the inference-only guarantee changes in kind rather than degree: an `LLMClient<inference>` node has no tool-calling *import*, not merely no tool-calling method, and a component declaring a capability it was not granted cannot be instantiated at all — the refusal lands before any guest code runs. Two properties follow from the boundary being typed rather than byte-oriented, and both are asserted by tests rather than argued in prose.

*There is no ambient authority to reach for, as a property of the artifact rather than of the host's configuration.* An earlier iteration of this tier compiled nodes to `wasm32-wasip1` modules run under an empty WASI context — no preopens, sockets, environment, or clock. That confined them, but the modules still *imported* `environ_get`, `fd_write`, and `path_open`; the imports were present and merely powerless, so confinement was a fact about how the host had configured the runtime, and a misconfigured host would have silently granted them. Compiled for `wasm32-unknown-unknown` and linked with no WASI adapter, no filesystem, socket, environment, or clock function appears among the imports at all — the hostile component imports nothing whatsoever. What was a configured absence is now a structural one, and the suite asserts the import set directly. A `wasip2` build would reintroduce the problem through `wasi:cli/*`, which is why the tier targets a bare component rather than a WASI snapshot.

*The boundary cannot silently drift from the node's declared signature.* Because a component's imports are typed interfaces rather than opaque symbols, the import set the runtime will accept is *derived from the graph*: each node's expected interfaces are computed from its `with` clause in the canonical JSON and compared against what the built component actually imports. A world that grants a node an interface its signature never requested fails that check rather than shipping as a quiet over-grant.

*The absence is per-node and derived, not tier-wide — and a deliberately granted clock is the demonstration.* The no-ambient-authority statement above is about what a node's clause does _not_ name, and it acquired its sharpest test when one clause _did_. The capability vocabulary now extends past what the customer-support graph needed, with three I/O kinds ahead of the service that will hold them: `Clock`, `HTTPClient<[host, ...]>` — whose scope is the first that is a _set_ rather than a mode or a name, narrowing by inclusion at composition (@sec:validator) — and `Notifier<'channel'>`. `Clock` is granted as the upstream `wasi:clocks/wall-clock` interface directly, which makes the recognition of @sec:sandboxing — that WASI's interfaces are one instance of capability-as-interface — operational rather than argued: a node declaring `Clock` imports exactly #ev.tiers.clock_node_imports.map(i => raw(i)).join([, ]), while its count of _ambient_ imports (imports its clause did not grant) is #ev.tiers.clock_node_ambient_imports, because the boundary between capability and ambient authority is the `with` clause, not the package namespace. Granting the clock cost the derivation no special case: each new kind is one mapping entry, and the same graph-to-binary comparison reads the three through the mechanism unchanged (@tab:derivation), which is what lets @sec:eval-derivation report the gate's coverage as a count of kinds actually checked rather than as the size of the mapping table. The allowlist of an `HTTPClient` lives in the handle behind the interface, exactly as a tool-LLM's tool scope does, so a confined node holds the _operation_ while the host holds the _scope_ — the enforcement consequences are measured in @sec:eval-tiers.

The same typing absorbed two node-body checks into types. Under the earlier flat `(ptr, len)` ABI, `ParseMessage` received an intent label as a string and had to *check* it against the permitted set so that adversarial text could not widen it; expressed as a WIT `enum`, widening is not representable, the check is gone, and the property is held by the type rather than by the node's own diligence. The model's reply is likewise a WIT `variant`, so a malformed reply has no encoding.

On the demonstrated customer path, #ev.injection.confined_count of its #ev.injection.path_length nodes run as components — every node but the pure `ReceiveMessage` narrowing — while the platform around them runs on the host tier, so the two tiers demonstrably compose within one graph (the incremental-migration path from opaque host node to confined node) and a majority of the taken path is confined rather than two isolated nodes. Each runs from a Rust body regenerated from the same signature and contract as its Python counterpart, with the graph unchanged — a concrete instance of the code-as-compiled-artifact property, the same contract with a different implementation language. Crucially the set now spans the typed capability boundary and not only pure transformations: `FetchContext` holds a read-only knowledge-base handle, `GenerateResponse` a scoped tool set plus that handle, and `SendReply` an identity-routed send whose single crossing lands on exactly the channel instance the parent routed, the confirmation carrying that instance's identity back across the WIT boundary. What interchangeability across languages then means is bounded by what a contract can say: these bodies are held to tests and to the closed vocabulary of @sec:contracts, not to a specification that pins their behaviour (@sec:open).

What this tier does not provide is memory-level unforgeability. Enforcement is unforgeable at the WASM boundary; a sandbox escape via a runtime defect is out of scope, and hardware-backed enforcement (CHERI) remains a named follow-up (@sec:open). Nor does typing the boundary touch the residual free-text channel — @sec:eval-injection reports that residual, measured on this tier on purpose.

== Capability identity, revocation, and rotation <sec:identity>

Revocation has a prerequisite: revoking a *specific* handle first requires a way to *name* it. The runtime's default is to provision one handle per declared capability *type*, shared across every node whose `with` clause names it, so two nodes each declaring `DBHandle<'knowledge-base', read>` receive the same object and there is no instance to revoke without severing both. For read-only handles this is harmless; for stateful, rate-limited, or revocable handles it is not. Capability *identity*, not merely capability type, must therefore be expressible at the graph boundary.

It now is, and in the graph source rather than only through an assembly API: each node may carry a `capability_identities` map binding a declared capability type to an identity label. A capability with no identity declared keeps the type-shared default, so existing graphs are unchanged. Because identity lives in the JSON, the validator checks it — an identity declared for a capability a node does not hold is rejected at validation time — and the generated pseudocode and diagrams render it, so a distinct instance is visible in the artifact and cannot drift from the source. That two same-typed handles carry independent state is a tested property.

On that prerequisite, revocation and rotation are built as one mechanism. An instance provisioned as *revocable* is wrapped in a caretaker @miller_robust_2006: the node holds a forwarding proxy, and a separate revoke authority — kept by the host that assembled the graph, never handed to a node — severs it, after which the node's next use fails loudly instead of exercising authority. *Rotation* re-points that same proxy at a new resource behind the same identity, guarded to a same-kind replacement so the surface the node holds cannot change kind underneath it. Revoke and rotate are two levers on one indirection rather than two mechanisms. Both are *targeted* (severing one identity leaves its same-typed siblings, and the type-shared default, untouched) and *opt-in* (an instance not declared revocable is provisioned bare), and both are tested.

Enforcement reaches the confined tier as well: a sandboxed node reaches its capabilities through typed WIT host functions backed by the very same caretaker, so severing it makes the guest's next capability crossing fail at the boundary — and there it is unforgeable, because the confined node's import set is its whole world and it has no other path to the resource. On the host tier the caveat is unchanged: severing binds the handle's authority, but not a node that reaches around the object model. Sandbox-tier *rotation*, and the graph-transformation form of both operations, remain undesigned — operations-adjacent work the migration story needs, carried in the corpus's forward agenda @lavi_predicting_2026.

Capability identity also gives the *principal* a place to live, which is what the confused-deputy argument of @sec:security needs and previously did not have. A run may bind a principal at assembly — the authenticated party the work is done for — and a node may declare `binds_principal` in the graph source, mirroring `discharges_trust` exactly: it is the sole marker licensing a node to extend the chain of parties acting on that principal's behalf, so an acting-on-behalf-of hop is a reviewable fact about the graph rather than a decision inside a node body. The trace then records, per node and at every altitude, the principal and that chain, in the shape of the delegation claim of #cite(<rfc8693>), which distinguishes acting-for from impersonation. Three properties follow and are tested: a graph that binds no principal behaves and serialises exactly as before, so the feature is opt-in at the call site; the chain grows only at declared binders, and only for work downstream of them; and the principal crosses a composition boundary with the routed handles, so a sub-graph acts for whoever its parent acts for and cannot mint a principal of its own — the same argument as @sec:subgraph, for the same reason, since the child is assembled with the parent's principal rather than provisioning one. The validator rejects a binder holding no capability, on the same ground it rejects a discharge with no untrusted input: a marker that cannot bind anything is a graph mistake, not a runtime surprise.

What this does *not* do is put the principal into the type grammar. A capability's scope is bound at the instance and recorded in the trace; it is not written in the capability's type, so the validator cannot yet answer statically what set of principals a given node could ever act as. That is the fuller design the authorisation literature points to (@sec:open), and it is where this stops.

Revocation being opt-in leaves a second question that the mechanism above does not answer: what bounds a granted handle when nobody remembers to sever it? The demonstrator's answer is borrowed rather than invented. Effect's resource discipline (@sec:effects) finalises whatever was acquired within a scope when that scope exits, making release structural instead of remembered, and an assembly can be used the same way: leaving its scope severs every revocable instance it provisioned, on the failure path as well as the success path, so authority granted for a run does not outlive the run. Assemblies used without a scope are unaffected, which keeps the bound opt-in at the call site. The limit is where this differs from what it borrows, and it is pinned by a test rather than left to the prose: scope exit reaches exactly the instances provisioned *revocable*, because an instance provisioned bare has no caretaker to sever and survives the scope. Closing that gap means a caretaker on every instance and therefore a proxy on every capability crossing — a design decision with a measurable price (@sec:eval-overhead), not an oversight.

== Operational composition: sub-graph execution <sec:subgraph>

Hierarchical composition is operational, not merely structural. A node whose name resolves to another graph is a *sub-graph node*, which the runtime executes by nested assembly and execution: the same assembly gate, the same executor. Composition therefore adds no second execution model — a sub-graph is a node whose body happens to be a graph.

`SupportPlatform` accordingly runs rather than merely assembles. A customer request entering at its `HTTPRoute<'platform:*'>` boundary is dispatched by `RouteRequest`, crosses into the #(ev.graphs.customer_support_nodes)-node `CustomerSupport` graph, and its outcome crosses back to `RecordAudit`, which appends it to the audit log through an append-only handle that cannot read the log back. Four properties of that crossing distinguish composition from mere call nesting, and all four are tested on the shipped graph.

+ *A sub-graph cannot provision authority of its own.* The executor holds no backend to provision from, so the child receives exactly the handles the parent routed and nothing else. Capability confinement across the composition boundary is a property of the plumbing rather than a rule to be remembered — the case we most expected to need enforcing turned out not to need it, because there was no mechanism by which it could fail.
+ *Identity routing has an executable consequence.* The distinct `customer_session` instance the platform declares for `CustomerSupport` is the instance the reply node *inside* that sub-graph sends on, and its sibling's instance is untouched.
+ *The parent matches the sub-graph's flat parameter list by position and type.* This is option (i) of the capability-routing question the vision left open; it is now settled at the runtime, while the fuller slot surface remains a language-design choice (@sec:open).
+ *Composition crosses enforcement tiers.* A host-tier `SupportPlatform` runs `CustomerSupport` with its nodes on the confined tier, the child's nodes resolving to their own tiers exactly as in a top-level run. This needs no new mechanism, which is itself the observation: because the executor holds no backend, confinement across the boundary holds unchanged whichever tier a child node runs on, and severing a routed instance at the parent makes the confined crossing fail two altitudes down — nothing on the child side can re-mint the severed capability.

The demonstration is bounded, and the bounds are worth stating rather than leaving to be discovered. Only leaf nodes on the taken path have implementations: `AgentDashboard` and `BillingService` are neither defined as graphs nor registered as node bodies, so those branches do not run. A sub-graph run reaching several terminals has no single boundary value, and the runtime *refuses* it rather than electing a winner — the multi-terminal aggregation question stays open, and fails loudly instead of silently.

The output side of a sub-graph's signature is checked, and the check is *structural, not nominal*. The convention @sec:concrete-graph adopts is that a service's boundary output is the union of the sub-graph's terminal types; an obvious implementation asserts a name for that union in the graph source, which is what the demonstrator first did — and a name the language has no alias mechanism to resolve is a name nothing can check, so a sub-graph node could declare any output type whatsoever and no analysis would object. The canonical graph therefore spells the union out, and the cross-graph analysis requires a sub-graph node's declared boundary output to equal the union of the referenced graph's terminal output types. A node narrowing that union — claiming only `DeliveryConfirmation` and hiding the escalation path — is now rejected at validation, with every edge still type-checking, so the fault is caught by the output-side check rather than as an edge type mismatch; a mutation-corpus case pins that verdict and its reason class.

== Behavioural contracts, at a deliberately small size <sec:contracts>

A node signature says what a body consumes and what authority it holds; it says nothing about what the body must *do*. Without that, "this implementation satisfies its specification" reduces to "its tests passed" — a weaker and differently-shaped claim, and the one that bounds the interchangeability argument of @sec:established. Nodes may now carry pre- and postconditions, and the size of the language is the design decision rather than an implementation detail.

The vocabulary is closed: comparison against a literal, membership in a declared variant set, field presence, and length. No quantifiers, no recursion, no arithmetic beyond `len`, and no escape into the host language. That ceiling is what separates a contract from a refinement type: the checker stays small enough to remain dependency-free, and a precondition drawn from an enumerable language is the kind a property-based-test strategy can later be *derived* from, which is how `icontract-hypothesis` avoids writing the same constraint twice. What it cannot express is equally definite — a postcondition relating output length to input length is outside it, and a language that could express that would need a solver.

Two properties are worth reporting. The first is *blame*, the load-bearing idea taken from Racket's contract system: a failed precondition and a failed postcondition are different events, indicting the wiring that supplied the value and the body that produced it respectively, and reporting which is which is most of the practical value because it names the thing to fix. The second is where each failure lands. A contract outside the vocabulary is rejected before anything runs — by the graph validator syntactically, and at assembly once parsed — so an unevaluatable specification fails the same gate an unsafe wiring does. That case is a corpus entry (@sec:eval-corpus) pinned to its own reason class, and it is there for a specific reason: the layer's value depends on the ceiling being real, since a predicate that quietly evaluated to false rather than being rejected would make every *satisfied* contract ambiguous, which is worse than having no contracts at all.

What this does not do is verify anything. Contracts are checked on the values a run actually produces, not proved over all inputs; the generative mode that would fuzz a node body against its own precondition is designed and unbuilt (@sec:open).

== The execution trace <sec:trace>

Every run now emits a structured, machine-readable *trace*: per node in execution order, the enforcement tier that ran it, the trust labels of its input and output, and the distinct capability crossings it made — each recorded as the typed interface crossed and the capability instance it landed on. Sub-graph runs nest, so a composed execution is inspectable at both altitudes. The format is pinned by a committed JSON Schema (`poc/trace-schema.json`) and its structure is deterministic: two runs of the same graph and input serialise identically, because nothing structural carries a timestamp and timing lives in one optional field excluded from comparison. This makes the run itself a checked artifact in the repository's established pattern — a rendered output generated from a checked source — and it is the artifact the graph inspector consumes, which keeps that interface a pure renderer rather than a second source of truth.

A crossing is recorded as `(interface, instance)` and deduplicated per node, not as a per-call log, and that choice is load-bearing for a claim the two tiers would otherwise only appear to satisfy. The host tier calls a capability method once; the confined tier's component runs its own internal loop, crossing the same interface a different number of times under a different function name. Recording the _set_ of interfaces a node crossed, and the instance each landed on, is the one representation both tiers produce identically — the host tier at the injected handle wrappers, the confined tier where the guest calls back across the WIT boundary through those same handles — and a test asserts the two traces are structurally equal once the tier field, the one thing that legitimately differs, is set aside. Instance attribution is what makes identity routing observable in the trace rather than only at assembly: the crossing the reply node makes inside `CustomerSupport` is recorded against the `customer_session` instance the platform routed, not the bare channel scope it would carry standalone. On the confined tier a node body cannot forge or suppress an entry, because recording sits at the boundary it cannot reach around; on the host tier recording is exactly as circumventable as every other host-tier guarantee, and the trace claims nothing stronger there.

The evaluation harness emits canonical traces of the prompt-injection scenario on both tiers into `dist/`, and pins their structural properties in the same build-failing style as the corpus verdicts. Two properties are pinned. First, trust is raised only at the declared discharge node (#raw(ev.trace.discharge_node)): a node that turned an untrusted input into a trusted output anywhere else would fail the build. Second — and this is the free-text residual of @sec:eval-injection made visible in data rather than only in prose — the untrusted taint reaches the tool-capable node (#raw(ev.trace.tool_capable_node)) through a _permitted_ field even on the confined tier: the node runs confined and its input is labelled _trusted_ (the `Untrusted<_>` wrapper was discharged upstream), yet adversarial text is still present in the question field it received. Pinning this keeps stronger enforcement from being misread as a stronger claim; the guarantee remains attenuation, not elimination.

A structured log of crossings is a prerequisite for replay, and it is worth saying precisely why this one is not sufficient for it. Durable-execution systems that do replay — Temporal @temporal_2025, Restate @restate_2025, and, closest to this architecture, Golem @golem_cloud_2026, which journals every host-function call across a component's WIT boundary and replays by re-injecting recorded results — record *actual values, per call, in call order*. The deduplicated view above records interface identity with no values and no realised resolution of any non-deterministic choice: the right kind of artifact at the wrong granularity. No replay is attempted here (@sec:open).

That granularity is not an oversight, which is what makes it interesting. Deduplication is exactly what buys the two-tier equality of the previous paragraph, so the two properties are in tension: a per-call journal diverges between the tiers by construction, because the confined tier's internal loop genuinely is a different execution from the host tier's single call. The resolution is that they answer different questions for different readers — an auditor asking what authority a node exercised, a debugger asking what happened on one run — and the runtime records both. Each capability use is journalled in encounter order with the interface, the instance, the operation reached for, and its position in the node's call sequence; the deduplicated view is *computed from* that journal by forgetting operation and sequence. Making it a projection rather than a second recorder is the substantive part: the structural equality of the two tiers' traces is a property of the projection rather than a coincidence between two views maintained by hand, and the journal itself is excluded from that comparison precisely because the tiers ought to differ there. Two limits are stated with it and pinned by tests. The journal captures ordering and multiplicity, not argument and return *values*, so it remains insufficient for replay on its own. And on the host tier it is exactly as circumventable as every other host-tier guarantee — a more detailed artifact is not a stronger one, so it is confined-tier-authoritative and host-tier-advisory.

The tension appears to be ours alone: Golem @golem_cloud_2026 runs one substrate, and Restate journals developer-demarcated steps, so neither has had to make the same source unit produce comparable evidence under two structurally different runtimes. The cost of the migration story is a constraint the durable-execution literature has not had to solve, and the projection is what pays it.

The repository also ships a web *inspector* over the trace (`ui/`, @fig:inspector): it renders the canonical graphs served byte-for-byte, triggers runs server-side through the same assemble-and-execute path the tests use, and overlays the returned trace — per-node tier, edge trust labels, crossings with instance names, nested sub-graph drill-down — with the mutation corpus runnable interactively and its pinned reason classes displayed. It is a pure consumer of the two checked artifacts, and every behaviour just named is covered by a contract test or an end-to-end check; the corpus build needs no Node toolchain. It is reported here rather than as a contribution because it demonstrates nothing the trace and the validator do not already establish — and because it *views and runs* graphs without authoring them, it is not the visual editor the vision predicted, which remains untouched, as does the agent workflow around it — both agenda items rather than results @lavi_predicting_2026.

