= Evaluation <sec:evaluation>

This section evaluates the demonstrator along five dimensions. The first is the central claim: whether a node's compiled component imports what its `with` clause grants and nothing else. The rest follow it — which faulty graphs the static analyses reject and by which analysis, what a capability-boundary crossing costs against the envelope the vision asserted without evidence, what an adversarial message can reach once it is inside the graph, and what each enforcement tier actually stops.

*Method.* Every figure in this section is interpolated from a build artifact emitted by a single run of the evaluation harness, which imports the mutation corpus, the benchmark, and the injection scenario rather than re-deriving them. No number below is transcribed by hand. The harness is a regression guard rather than a report: each corpus case pins the verdict it must produce *and the reason class it must be caught by*, and any divergence is rejected instead of rewriting the table. Pinning the reason matters more than pinning the verdict — a pass/fail pin would stay green if trust laundering silently began to be caught as an ordinary type mismatch, which is precisely the regression that would hollow out the central claim. The corpus also pins itself against silent growth: adding an unsafe variant without pinning it is an error, not an uncounted pass.

*Scope.* The corpus is *curated and illustrative*. It contains the faults we thought to write down, and the counts below report how many of those were caught. That is evidence the graph-level analyses are implementable and catch the mistakes they target; it is not a soundness result, and no claim is made that the corpus is exhaustive or that an uncaught class does not exist. @sec:threats states this and the other limits in full.

The overhead figures are wall-clock timings from one machine, reported to establish an order of magnitude rather than as portable benchmarks. They were produced on #raw(ev.environment.platform) (#raw(ev.environment.processor)), Python #raw(ev.environment.python), wasmtime #raw(ev.environment.wasmtime).

== The capability surface, derived and checked <sec:eval-derivation>

The central claim first, because it is the one an over-granting world would falsify. For each node ported to the confined tier, the permitted interface set is computed from its `with` clause in the canonical graph — parsed with the same type parser the validator uses — and compared against what its built component actually imports.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, left, right, center),
    stroke: (x, y) => if y == 0 { (bottom: 0.6pt) } else { none },
    table.header([*node*], [*component*], [*capabilities granted*], [*binary matches*]),
    ..ev.derivation.nodes.map(n => (
      [#raw(n.node)#if not n.shipped [#super[†]]],
      raw(n.component),
      str(n.granted_count),
      tick(n.agrees),
    )).flatten()
  ),
  caption: [The `with` clause held against the compiled artifact. Counts exclude the shared type vocabulary every component links against, which grants no authority and is compared like the rest. † marks the one row whose graph does not ship: `Heartbeat` is the I/O-kind demonstrator of @sec:sandbox-tier, read through the same derivation as the rest and reported here rather than left in the test suite, because it is what carries three of the capability kinds through this gate.],
) <tab:derivation>

#ev.derivation.agreeing of #ev.derivation.total components import exactly what the graph grants them, across #ev.derivation.grants_compared capability grants. The comparison is a build gate rather than a report: divergence in *either* direction fails, an extra import being the over-grant the claim is about and a missing one meaning the derivation has drifted ahead of the artifact. `GenerateResponse` is the row that carries the most weight — the tool-capable node, and the only ported node holding two capabilities — because it is where an over-grant would matter and where the graph's distinction between an inference client and a scoped tool client becomes an import-table fact.

The honest reading is narrow in the same way the corpus's is, and one distinction inside it is worth making precisely, because conflating the two numbers would overstate the result. The comparison above has been *exercised* on #ev.derivation.kinds_covered_count of the #ev.tiers.capability_kinds capability kinds this tier models; #ev.derivation.kinds_unexercised.map(k => raw(k)).join([, ], last: [ and ]) is mapped but held by no ported node, so it is checked by nothing here. The size of the capability-to-interface mapping is what the derivation *could* map; only the table is what it has been held against a binary for. What the table establishes, then, is that the derivation is implementable, that it covers every ported node rather than a chosen example, and that adding a kind cost no special case — on a hand-curated vocabulary in one repository. It is not evidence that the derivation generalises to capability kinds nobody has written a mapping for, and the mapping itself is the piece a wrong entry would silently corrupt — a risk @sec:threat-construct states.

== Graph-mutation corpus <sec:eval-corpus>

Each case is a graph the validator either accepts or rejects at assembly time, before any node executes. The canonical graphs must be *accepted*: a validator that rejected everything would catch every unsafe wiring and be worthless, so the safe cases are corpus entries in their own right rather than a formality.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, left, left, left, center),
    stroke: (x, y) => if y == 0 { (bottom: 0.6pt) } else { none },
    table.header([*case*], [*kind*], [*verdict*], [*caught by*], []),
    ..ev.corpus.cases.map(c => (
      raw(c.name),
      c.kind,
      c.actual,
      if c.reason == none { [—] } else { c.reason },
      tick(c.ok),
    )).flatten()
  ),
  caption: [The graph-mutation corpus. Each faulty case is pinned to the analysis that must catch it, not merely to the fact of rejection. All but `unevaluatable_contract` are unsafe wirings; that one is a specification the checker refuses.],
) <tab:corpus>

#ev.corpus.canonical_accepted/#ev.corpus.canonical_total safe graphs were accepted and #ev.corpus.mutation_rejected/#ev.corpus.mutation_total faulty ones rejected at assembly time, each by its pinned reason class.

Two of the mutations separate a blunt mistake from a subtle one. `bypass_pipeline` wires untrusted input straight into the tool-capable node; the edge simply does not type-check on data shape, and any structural checker would catch it. `launder_trust` is the interesting case: it *repairs* that mismatch by widening the tool-capable node's declared input to `Untrusted<RawMessage>`, after which every edge in the graph is data-compatible. It is nonetheless rejected, as an upward coercion — the node raises trust without being a declared discharger — and it is rejected by the trust lattice rather than by edge typing, which is the property the pin enforces.

The other two extend the same discipline past edge wiring. `mislabel_subgraph_output` is the composition analogue of `launder_trust`: a service sub-graph node declares a boundary output narrower than the union its terminals emit, hiding the escalation path while every edge still type-checks, and it is pinned to the output-side cross-graph check rather than to edge typing (@sec:subgraph). `unevaluatable_contract` is a different kind of fault altogether — not an unsafe wiring but an unevaluatable specification, a precondition outside the closed vocabulary of @sec:contracts — and it is in the corpus because the contract layer's value depends on that ceiling being real (@sec:contracts).

This is the corpus's whole point, and its limit. It establishes that the distinction between "type-checks" and "is well-trusted" is real, implementable, and load-bearing on a graph that would otherwise pass, and that the same discipline extends to a composition boundary and to a specification the checker cannot evaluate. It does not establish that no further kind of mistake exists.

== Capability-boundary overhead <sec:eval-overhead>

The vision asserted a working envelope it had no numbers for: if a node performs on the order of #d.envelope_node_work_ms ms of useful work, a per-crossing cost below roughly #d.envelope_crossing_ms ms keeps total overhead under about #d.envelope_max_overhead. These are the numbers, whichever way they fall.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, right, left),
    stroke: (x, y) => if y == 0 { (bottom: 0.6pt) } else { none },
    table.header([*measurement*], [*value*], [*notes*]),
    [component compilation], [#d.compilation_ms ms], [one-time per artifact, cached; not paid per call],
    [instantiation (per node)], [#d.instantiation_ms ms], [the tier's fixed per-node price; pooling would amortise it],
    [`ParseMessage` run (warm)], [#d.parse_invocation_ms ms], [#ev.overhead.parse_crossings crossing],
    [`GenerateResponse` run (warm)], [#d.generate_invocation_ms ms], [#ev.overhead.generate_crossings crossings],
    [*marginal per-crossing*], [*#d.crossing_us µs*], [differenced between two warm paths through one component],
  ),
  caption: [Capability-boundary overhead on the confined tier. Every crossing lifts and lowers typed WIT values.],
) <tab:overhead>

A crossing costs #d.crossing_us µs — #if ev.overhead.within_envelope [within] else [*outside*] the #d.envelope_crossing_ms ms envelope, projecting to #d.projected_overhead overhead on a node doing #d.envelope_node_work_ms ms of work. The measurement was worth making because the typed boundary is not obviously free: the tier's first implementation passed a packed `(ptr, len)` pair into linear memory and let both sides parse the bytes, whereas every crossing measured here marshals typed records, enums, lists, and variants. Typing the boundary did not cost the performance argument.

Two cautions bound the reading. First, these figures should *not* be compared like-for-like with those previously reported for the flat-ABI tier, because the benchmark's timing discipline was corrected at the same time: it now warms each measurement and takes the best of several rounds, where before it timed a single cold pass and so charged JIT and interpreter warm-up to whatever it measured first. Since the per-crossing cost is derived by *differencing* two timings, that defect could distort it by an order of magnitude in either direction. The comparison that survives is the one that matters: typing the boundary did not move the crossing out of its order of magnitude. Second, these are proof-of-concept figures from a Python host and a two-node slice, not a production runtime at graph scale. They establish that the crossing itself is cheap and locate the fixed cost in instantiation; they say nothing about serialisation cost for complex types, or about graphs with hundreds of nodes.

== Prompt-injection attenuation <sec:eval-injection>

An adversarial message instructing the model to call an `exfiltrate` tool is driven through the `CustomerSupport` graph. What matters is not whether the model is fooled — assume it is — but what it can *reach* once fooled. The message traverses #ev.injection.path.len() nodes: #ev.injection.path.map(n => raw(n)).join([ → ]), of which #ev.injection.tiers.values().filter(t => t == "sandbox").len() run on the confined tier and the rest on the host tier.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, left, center),
    stroke: (x, y) => if y == 0 { (bottom: 0.6pt) } else { none },
    table.header([*property*], [*result*], []),
    [the tool-capable node received], raw(ev.injection.received_type), tick(not ev.injection.is_untrusted),
    [it received an `Untrusted<_>` value], yesno(ev.injection.is_untrusted), tick(not ev.injection.is_untrusted),
    [an out-of-scope tool call was refused], yesno(ev.injection.out_of_scope_call_refused), tick(ev.injection.out_of_scope_call_refused),
    [adversarial text still present in a permitted field], yesno(ev.injection.adversarial_text_present), [—],
  ),
  caption: [Prompt-injection outcome. The last row is the residual, and it is asserted on the *confined* tier deliberately.],
) <tab:injection>

*The residual, stated plainly.* The last row is not a failure; it is the boundary of the claim. The `Untrusted<RawMessage>` value is consumed at the parse boundary and never reaches the tool-capable node, which receives a #raw(ev.injection.received_type) instead. But the question text itself remains a free-text field, and that field stays adversarial data. The guarantee is therefore *attenuation, not elimination*: the model can still be influenced by that text; it cannot call anything outside `{lookup}`, because the handle refuses. Blast radius drops from arbitrary tool execution to a bad lookup query.

Two things follow that are easy to get wrong in the reading. The confined tier does *not* close this residual — what bounds the damage is the capability scope, not the sandbox — which is why the assertion is made on that tier on purpose, so that stronger enforcement is not misread as a stronger claim. And the attenuation depends on schema discipline, not on the framework alone: a discharging type that retains unbounded free text in a position flowing to a privileged node gives most of it back. The framework supplies the enforcement substrate; disciplined schema design is what makes use of it.

== Enforcement tiers: what each one stops <sec:eval-tiers>

The same escape attempts were run on both tiers. The host-tier column is *expected* to read ESCAPES: host discipline gives a node only its declared handles, but nothing stops a hostile node from reaching around the object model. That gap is the reason the confined tier exists, and it is reported here rather than omitted.

#figure(
  table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, center, center, center),
    stroke: (x, y) => if y == 0 { (bottom: 0.6pt) } else { none },
    table.header([*escape attempt*], [*host tier*], [*confined tier*], []),
    ..ev.tiers.escapes.map(e => (
      e.probe,
      if e.host_escapes { [ESCAPES] } else { [—] },
      if e.sandbox_escapes { [*ESCAPED*] } else { [denied] },
      tick(not e.sandbox_escapes),
    )).flatten()
  ),
  caption: [Escape attempts on both tiers. The host tier's failures are the recorded gap, not an omission.],
) <tab:tiers>

The ungranted-capability row is the sharpest: the component imports an interface it was never granted, so it cannot instantiate at all — the refusal lands before any guest code runs. And on the confined tier the capability is *absent, not merely unexposed*. An inference-only node's import set holds only #ev.tiers.inference_node_imports.map(i => raw(i)).join([, ]), with #ev.tiers.ambient_imports filesystem, socket, environment, or clock imports in it. Confinement is a property of the artifact rather than of how the host happened to configure it — the distinction @sec:sandbox-tier is built to make.

The clock and HTTP rows widen the evidence past what the customer-support graph needed, and they are each other's dual. A hostile body granted _only_ a clock — one deliberately granted WASI interface (@sec:sandbox-tier) — still cannot open a socket or read a file: the grant bought the authority it names and reopened nothing ambient. And a node granted an allowlisted HTTP capability is refused at the crossing when it asks for a host outside its list — the shape of an out-of-scope tool call, because the component holds the operation while the allowlist lives in the handle. Both kinds reach the derivation gate of @sec:eval-derivation rather than only these two probes: the `Heartbeat` row of @tab:derivation is where the three I/O kinds are held against a binary, which is why a graph that does not ship is reported there. That brings the gate's exercised coverage to #ev.derivation.kinds_covered_count of the #ev.tiers.capability_kinds kinds the tier models, up from the #ev.derivation.kinds_covered_shipped_count carried by the shipped graph's ported nodes. It is still a hand-curated vocabulary in one repository and not evidence that the derivation covers arbitrary kinds, but the growth cost no special case, which is the property that was untested either way before.

*Fidelity.* Enforcement is unforgeable at the WASM boundary, not at the memory level; CHERI remains a named follow-up (@sec:open). Only the nodes ported to the confined tier get it — the rest run on the host tier, which demonstrates the shape of confinement rather than enforcing it. That the two tiers compose in one graph is the incremental-migration path, and it is the reason the host tier's gap is a transitional state rather than a defect of the model.

= What this establishes, and what it does not <sec:established>

Stated plainly, because the failure mode of a paper like this one is not a false claim but a true claim read more broadly than it was made.

*Established.* A node's declared capabilities determine the authority surface of its compiled artifact: the permitted import set is computed from the `with` clause in the canonical graph and compared against the built component for every node ported to the confined tier (@tab:derivation), and a world granting an interface the signature never requested fails that comparison. Because the components carry no WASI adapter, the absence of filesystem, socket, environment, and clock authority is a property of the binary rather than of the host's configuration — a distinction we could only draw after building the tier both ways, and only the second of which survives a misconfigured host. The trust lattice rejects the laundering graph in which *every edge type-checks*, and rejects it as a lattice violation rather than a type mismatch, which is the pinned property rather than a coincidence of this implementation. Crossings cost tens of microseconds while marshalling typed values. Sub-graph composition executes without a second execution model, and a sub-graph cannot provision authority of its own — not because a rule forbids it, but because the executor holds no backend from which it could. Contracts are stated in a vocabulary small enough that a specification the checker cannot evaluate is refused rather than silently passed, and a failure names whether the wiring or the body is at fault.

*Not established, and not to be read in.* Nothing here is a soundness result: the validator implements a lattice, and no argument connects graphs well-typed under it to noninterference. Every security claim above is a claim about *what the implemented checks reject*, not about what a sound type system would guarantee. The mutation corpus is curated, so its counts measure implementability and not coverage; a fully-caught curated corpus reads as a proof unless it says otherwise, and this one is not. Confinement is established for the nodes compiled to components and *fails* on the host tier around them, where the same escapes succeed and are recorded as passing tests. Prompt injection is attenuated and not eliminated: adversarial text reaches the tool-capable node in a permitted field on the confined tier, and what bounds the damage is the capability scope — not the type, and not the sandbox. A graph of #ev.graphs.customer_support_nodes nodes is not hundreds, and one domain is not many.

The prediction-by-prediction accounting of these results against the founding vision's claims — including which of them the vision got wrong, and the four design corrections building it produced — is the subject of the companion paper @lavi_predicting_2026, and is deliberately not restated here.

= Related work <sec:related>

Each element of this work has been explored independently; the convergence that makes their synthesis newly practical is the argument of @sec:intro. To our knowledge, no existing system simultaneously provides graph-level capability analysis, trust-propagated type checking across component boundaries, and generated implementations executing within capability-restricted sandboxes; the security-by-construction property emerges from their combination, not from any element alone. The one substantial addition to the prior art surveyed in the earlier paper is the WASM Component Model's interface layer (@sec:sandboxing), on which the confined tier of @sec:sandbox-tier is built directly.

== Securing LLM agents against prompt injection <sec:agentsec>

The threat this work attenuates is well characterised. Greshake et al. @greshake_not_2023 documented _indirect_ prompt injection against deployed LLM applications — adversarial instructions placed in content the model retrieves rather than in the user's own message — and derived a taxonomy of the resulting compromises. AgentDojo @debenedetti_agentdojo_2024 turned that threat into a benchmark: 97 realistic tool-using tasks and 629 security cases in a dynamic environment, built to evaluate attacks and defences against each other rather than in isolation.

Two lines of defence bear directly on this work. Beurer-Kellner et al. @beurer-kellner_design_2025 catalogue design patterns that trade agent generality for resistance — action-selector, plan-then-execute, dual-LLM, context-minimisation — each constraining what untrusted content can influence. The `CustomerSupport` graph instantiates several of them: the tool-capable node receives only a `ConversationContext` assembled downstream of moderation, and the nodes that read raw user text hold inference-only clients. The difference is that here they are not patterns a developer applies but consequences of the wiring, checkable at assembly time; the cost is that the graph cannot express agent behaviours those patterns exclude.

Closest is CaMeL @debenedetti_defeating_2025, which shares this work's central intuition — that the defence belongs in the _system around_ the model rather than in the model — and, strikingly, reaches for the same currency: capabilities. CaMeL extracts control and data flow from the trusted query, runs untrusted data through a quarantined LLM with no tool access, and enforces capability-based policies over value provenance in a custom interpreter before each tool call, solving 77% of AgentDojo tasks with provable security against 84% undefended. Three differences locate the present work rather than rank it. CaMeL enforces at *runtime*, per tool call, inside one agent's execution; the signal graph enforces *statically*, before any node runs, over a composed system whose sub-graphs are themselves analysed. CaMeL's policies govern data provenance; the graph's `with` clauses additionally govern *authority topology* — which component may reach which resource in which mode — independently of what data flows. And CaMeL's guarantees rest on its interpreter being correct and its LLM-generated plan being faithful; the graph's rest on a validator plus the component's import table, so a confined node's authority is bounded by its binary even if every analysis above it is wrong. The approaches are complementary: CaMeL constrains a dynamically-planned agent, while the signal graph presumes the topology is authored and reviewed in advance, which is a strong assumption CaMeL deliberately avoids. Neither closes the free-text residual of @sec:eval-injection.

Closest of all in mechanism is TACIT @odersky_securing_2026, which places the agent in a language-level safety harness: rather than calling tools, agents emit Scala 3 code under capture checking, so the type system statically prevents generated code from forging access rights or exceeding its capability budget, and a `Classified[T]` wrapper marks sensitive values that only designated mechanisms may discharge. The thesis is ours — that agent safety should be a property of the infrastructure rather than a bet on model behaviour — and the mechanism is the type-level capability tracking of @boruch-gruszecki_capturing_2023 applied to agent-authored code. Three differences locate this work rather than rank it, and the first two cut against us as often as for us. TACIT checks freeform imperative code as each snippet arrives; the signal graph validates a *fixed topology* before anything runs, which buys assembly-time analysis at the cost of the open-ended expressiveness TACIT retains. TACIT's capture checker is the only boundary and sits at the source-language level, which is why it ships a restricted subset to disable casts and reflection; the confined tier's import table binds the artifact even where the analysis above it is wrong, and is correspondingly narrower — it bounds authority, not behaviour. And `Classified[T]` is a property of one computation, whereas the trust lattice here propagates across a wiring, applied uniformly to edges and node bodies (@sec:validator). TACIT is also the more thorough evaluation: it reports task performance against tool-calling baselines on established agent benchmarks across several model families, where @sec:evaluation offers a curated corpus on one graph.

The comparison also names an omission. This work evaluates against a hand-written adversarial message on its own canonical graph, not against AgentDojo or any published attack suite; @sec:threat-construct records that the corpus is curated, and the existence of a benchmark for exactly this threat is the sharpest available statement of what that limitation costs.

== Architecture models that describe but do not constrain <sec:c4>

The Object Management Group's Model-Driven Architecture @omg_mda_2014 pursued a superficially similar vision — models as the primary artifact, code generated from them — and foundered on round-trip engineering: models and generated code diverged as soon as developers edited the code, and re-synchronising them cost more than maintaining the code alone. The signal graph avoids this structurally, being not a model _of_ the code but the program at the level humans review, with node implementations regenerable against the stable interface of the graph's signatures. The C4 model @brown_c4_2018 is the widely adopted lightweight successor (Structurizr, LikeC4, IcePanel, Mermaid), and recent work extends it toward the role envisaged here: LikeC4 @likec4_2025 exposes the model to agents as a queryable knowledge base, and some practitioners, including the author @lavi_c4_skill_2025, maintain C4 models as "executable context" constraining agent behaviour. C4's limit is the one this paper's title answers: it is a _communication_ model, not a _constraint_ one. It describes an authority distribution; nothing checks the deployed artifact against it. The derivation of @sec:sandbox-tier is precisely that missing check, at the cost of a far more restrictive notation.

== Effect systems, capabilities, and purity <sec:effects>

Haskell showed that purity-by-default with explicit effects is practical @peyton_jones_haskell_2003, and algebraic effect systems (Koka @leijen_type_2017, Frank @lindley_be_2017) and dependent types (Idris 2 @brady_idris_2021) make effects first-class and composable. Roc @feldman_roc_2025 is the closest language-level analogue to per-node capability injection: effects come only from an interchangeable "platform" the application cannot bypass, so the platform boundary _is_ the capability boundary — though at whole-application rather than per-component granularity. A more recent line makes capabilities the type-level currency for effects directly: Effekt @brachthauser_effects_2020 passes effect operations as capability values the type system tracks in scope, and Scala 3's capture checking @boruch-gruszecki_capturing_2023 records in a value's type which capabilities it _captures_. Both show capability tracking can be ergonomic inside a general-purpose type system, at function granularity within one program. This work applies the same principle one level up, at architectural components, with the runtime rather than the compiler as enforcer; showing that this coarser enforcement suffices for the security properties claimed is part of the verification obligation this work leaves open (@sec:open).

The discipline has also reached production practice, which matters for a design whose main adoption risk is its ergonomic cost. Effect @effect_ts_2026 — a TypeScript library carrying the design of Scala's ZIO into mainstream use, into which the `fp-ts` project merged — types every computation as `Effect<Success, Error, Requirements>`, where the third parameter is a type-level record of the services the computation needs and the computation *cannot be run* until every one of them is discharged. That is close to a node's `with` clause in both form and consequence: authority is declared in the signature rather than reached for from the environment, and a live service is swapped for a test double at the provisioning boundary with no change to the computation's own type. It goes further than the demonstrator in one respect worth naming, because it bears on an open question rather than a settled one: a `Layer<Out, E, In>` makes the *construction* of a service a typed, composable value that may itself require services and may itself fail, and composing layers discharges requirements incrementally. The demonstrator has no such algebra — it provisions one handle per declared capability type from a backend, with construction untyped and invisible in the graph — and that is precisely the surface on which the hierarchical capability-routing options of @sec:open differ.

The distance is exactly the one this paper is built to draw. Effect's requirements channel is a complete, type-checked account of what a computation depends on, and it enforces nothing: TypeScript has ambient authority, and no requirement prevents a computation from calling `fs.readFileSync` directly. It is the host tier of @sec:host-tier at language scale — the shape of the guarantee without an artifact that holds it. The library's users sometimes call that channel a set of capabilities, and the usage is worth resisting here: these are declared dependencies, not unforgeable references, and treating the two as one thing is the same conflation @sec:sandbox-tier had to be built twice to see. Nor does Effect propagate trust — schema validation yields a parsed type, not a provenance label, so there is no analogue of `Untrusted<T>` and no lattice — and an Effect program's dependency graph is assembled by composition as the program runs, rather than existing beforehand as an artifact a checker can reject, which is the assembly-time property of @sec:eval-corpus rather than a difference in expressiveness.

== Capability-based security <sec:capabilities>

The object-capability model @miller_robust_2006 @goos_paradigm_2003 makes possession of an unforgeable reference the proof of authorisation, and has been realised across the stack: languages (E @miller_robust_2006, Caja @google_caja_2012), operating systems (Capsicum @watson_capsicum_2010, seL4 @klein_sel4_2009), and runtimes (Deno @deno_2025, WASI). Most relevant is the _distributed_ treatment, where network reachability is itself ambient authority: E mediates all inter-object communication through explicitly passed references with no ambient network @miller_robust_2006, and Agoric's Hardened JavaScript @agoric_hardened_2023 brings the discipline to a mainstream language; Stiegler @stiegler_introduction_2010 gives an accessible account. The signal graph's explicit edge wiring is the architectural analogue of E's reference passing — a node not wired to a network capability has no mechanism for external communication — which the confined tier realises and the host tier does not (@sec:eval-tiers). Cedar @cutler_cedar_2024, a formally verified authorisation language, shows fine-grained analysable authority is production-viable. Pony @clebsch_deny_2015 is the closest capability-typed prior art in a concurrent language, integrating reference capabilities into the actor type system — but its capabilities govern _memory_ (aliasing, mutability), whereas the signal graph's govern _external authority_.

== CHERI: capabilities in hardware <sec:cheri>

CHERI @watson_cheri_2015 makes every pointer a hardware-checked capability carrying bounds, permissions, and a tag, so forgery traps in hardware with no software guard. It is reaching commercial maturity: Arm's Morello @arm_morello_2023, Microsoft's CHERIoT @amar_cheriot_2023 on embedded RISC-V, Codasip's commercial IP @codasip_cheri_2024, and SCI Semiconductor's ICENI @sci_iceni_2024, which reached first silicon in March 2026 @sci_first_silicon_2026; the CHERI Alliance @cheri_alliance_2024 coordinates adoption. Three properties matter here: unforgeable capabilities, fine-grained in-process compartmentalisation, and low porting cost — a 2021 study ported six million lines of C/C++ with changes to 0.026% of source lines @capabilities_limited_cheri_2021. CHERI would backstop the confined tier, preventing capability forgery at the memory level even under a sandbox memory-safety bug. No part of this work has run on CHERI hardware (@sec:open).

== Lightweight sandboxing and the WASM Component Model <sec:sandboxing>

WebAssembly provides capability-based isolation with near-native performance across languages @haas_bringing_2017, and the layer this work depends on directly is the _WASM Component Model_ @w3c_component_model_2024: components declare typed imports and exports (in the WIT interface language) that the runtime links with type-checked bindings. The _WebAssembly System Interface_ (WASI) is a standard _library_ of such interfaces — filesystems, clocks, sockets — built on it. That distinction is what @sec:sandbox-tier exploits: expressing each capability kind as its own WIT interface makes a node's `with` clause its component's import list, so the runtime boundary realises the node's typed signature rather than sitting beneath it as a byte protocol. WASI is itself a worked precedent for the pattern — granting each resource as an explicit typed interface, structurally the same object as `DBHandle<'knowledge-base', read>` applied to host resources — so this work does not adopt WASI so much as recognise its interfaces as one instance of capability-as-interface and extend the discipline to application capabilities WASI does not model.

This is the point at which the claim of @sec:claim must be stated narrowly, because the surrounding ecosystem already ships most of it. Declaring a component's authority rather than granting it ambiently is now the industry default: Fermyon Spin @fermyon_spin_2026 gives each component a manifest allowlist of outbound hosts; wasmCloud @wasmcloud_2026 declares links routing a component's WIT interface to a *named provider instance*, which is our capability identity (@sec:identity) in production; and WASI-Virt @bytecodealliance_wasi_virt_2026 composes a virtual adapter into a component to satisfy or physically *remove* its WASI imports, which already makes confinement a property of the artifact rather than of the host's configuration — the distinction drawn above, with tooling behind it. None of that is claimed as new here.

What we did not find in that ecosystem is the step this tier actually takes: deriving the permitted interface set from a *separate, higher-level architecture model* and gating the build on comparing it against the compiled binary. wasmCloud's manifests route against hand-authored WIT, and WAC's `wac targets` @bytecodealliance_wac_2026 checks a component against a hand-written world; in both the world is the source of truth and is maintained by hand. Deriving it from the graph makes the `with` clause the single source and the binary the thing held to account, so drift fails the build rather than being re-declared. That is a narrow claim and it should be read narrowly — this was a survey of published documentation rather than an audit of implementations, and the ecosystem moves quickly. A natural strengthening we have not implemented is to compose the unwanted imports away in the WASI-Virt manner *as well as* testing for them, so that drift is unforgeable rather than merely detected (@sec:open).

The layering also settles a longevity worry, since WASI's interfaces are still moving: 0.1's `wasm32-wasip1` snapshot is legacy, 0.2 rebased onto the Component Model, 0.3 @wasi_030_2026 added native async, and a stable 1.0 is expected @wasi_roadmap_2026. Every version rebases onto the same Component Model, so defining bespoke WIT interfaces against the substrate rather than any WASI snapshot leaves the tier untouched by that churn; where a node genuinely needs a host resource, the corresponding WASI interface is the right thing to grant it, as a capability like any other — which the tier now does for the wall clock (@sec:sandbox-tier). Beyond WASM, Monty @pydantic_monty_2026 reaches microsecond-scale startup with host isolation by default, BEAM processes start in single-digit microseconds, and CHERIoT demonstrates hardware compartmentalisation with negligible overhead.

== Adjacent traditions <sec:adjacent>

Four lines inform the design without competing with it. *Content-addressed code*: Unison @chiusano_unison_2025 stores code as hash-identified typed ASTs rather than text, and its "abilities" are an algebraic effect system in which a program performs only effects it has been given; the models are complementary, since Unison addresses storage and _what category_ of effect a function performs while the signal graph addresses explicit capability wiring and `Untrusted<T>` propagation — the fine-grained authority boundaries abilities do not track. Darklang @biggar_darklang_2019 showed real gains for a unified code-and-infrastructure environment but its proprietary substrate is a cautionary tale about migration paths, which is why this work layers above existing runtimes. *Typed holes*: Hazel @omar_hazelnut_2017 @omar_live_2019 keeps every editor state meaningful, and @blinn_statically_2024 finds that static context from a hole's type materially improves LLM generation quality — direct support for the claim that node-implementation agents benefit from typed signatures, and the closest existing evidence for the experiment @sec:conclusion proposes. *Process isolation*: the BEAM @armstrong_making_2003 is the closest existing runtime to the intended one, and the signal graph's isolated typed-message components are an instance of the actor model @hewitt_universal_1973 — which, as usually realised, leaves implicit both which external resources an actor may reach and the wiring, determined at runtime by message sends rather than statically. *Durable execution*: Temporal @temporal_2025, Restate @restate_2025, and Azure Durable Functions @microsoft_durable_functions_2025 validate at scale the mechanism the vision's replay claim assumed, managing nondeterministic interleaving by constraining application code or journalling each operation's outcome; replay at _capability-boundary crossings_ rather than workflow steps is the difference the trace of @sec:trace makes possible and does not implement (@sec:open).

== Spec-driven development <sec:sdd>

SDD frameworks — OpenSpec @fission_openspec_2025, Spec Kit @github_spec_kit_2025, Kiro @aws_kiro_2025 — persist versioned specification artifacts so agents have stable context instead of chat history, and Codespeak @breslav_codespeak_2025 compiles plain-English specs to code, treating implementation as generated — close to this work's stance, but its specs are untyped prose without capability or trust annotations, so the security-by-construction properties are outside its scope. Current frameworks treat spec and architecture as separate prose concerns; this work's argument is that as they mature their output should _become_ the typed graph. A parallel, less formal current supports the trajectory: visual automation platforms (Zapier @zapier_2025, Make.com @make_2025, n8n @n8n_2025) already let non-developers build directed graphs of actions, and agent-orchestration frameworks such as LangGraph @langchain_langgraph_2025 structure LLM applications as explicit state-transition graphs. What these lack is what this work adds — typed interfaces, capability restriction, trust propagation — and as teams scale from internal automations to customer-facing agents, that gap becomes the acute one.

= Limitations and open problems <sec:threats>

The demonstrator is small and the claim in @sec:claim was scoped to what it can carry. This section states the limits explicitly, because the failure mode of a paper like this one is not a false claim but a true claim read more broadly than it was made.

== Construct validity: what the corpus measures <sec:threat-construct>

*The corpus is curated, and counts are not coverage.* @tab:corpus reports that #ev.corpus.mutation_rejected of #ev.corpus.mutation_total faulty graphs were rejected. That ratio is #d.mutations_caught_pct by construction: the corpus contains the mistakes we thought to write down, and we wrote down mistakes we expected the analyses to catch. A fully-caught curated corpus reads as a soundness proof unless it says otherwise, and it is not one. The honest reading is narrow — the analyses are implementable and catch the classes they target — and no claim is made that an uncaught class does not exist.

*The derivation is only as right as its mapping table.* @tab:derivation compares each component against a set computed from the graph, and that computation runs through a hand-written mapping from capability type to WIT interface. The comparison catches a binary that drifts from the graph; it cannot catch a mapping entry that is wrong in both directions at once — a kind mapped to an interface more permissive than the type intends would produce an agreeing row and an over-granted node. What bounds this in practice is that the vocabulary is small and each entry is one line, which is an argument about reviewability rather than a check.

*Reason-class pinning mitigates but does not resolve this.* Requiring `launder_trust` to be caught by the trust lattice rather than by edge typing means the corpus cannot stay green while the mechanism under test quietly stops working. That is a real guard against rot, and it is why the pins exist. It says nothing about mistakes absent from the corpus.

*Two graphs, one domain.* Both canonical graphs describe an AI customer-support pipeline, chosen because untrusted input, graduated LLM access, and fine-grained capability distinctions are all visible in it. It is a domain selected to display the properties under test. Whether the analyses are as informative on graphs whose security structure is less legible is untested.

*The injection result is not benchmarked.* @sec:eval-injection drives one hand-written adversarial message through one graph. A benchmark for precisely this threat exists — AgentDojo @debenedetti_agentdojo_2024, at the scale given in @sec:agentsec — and this work does not use it, so the injection finding is a demonstration of the mechanism rather than a measured attack-success rate comparable with published defences. Evaluating a signal graph against that suite requires expressing its task environments as graphs, which is real work and is exactly the work that would make the attenuation claim quantitative.

== Internal validity: the measurements <sec:threat-internal>

*The overhead figures are from one machine and a two-node slice.* They are wall-clock timings on #raw(ev.environment.platform), reported to establish an order of magnitude, not as portable benchmarks. The supported claim is that a crossing costs tens of microseconds and therefore sits well inside the asserted envelope — not the specific figure.

*The per-crossing cost is a differenced quantity, which is fragile.* It is derived by differencing two warm timings through one component, so any systematic cost landing in one term and not the other is charged directly to the boundary. That this is not hypothetical is the subject of @sec:eval-overhead: an earlier benchmark timed a single cold pass, which was enough to distort the figure by an order of magnitude.

*Instantiation and compilation costs are reported but not amortised.* The per-node instantiation cost is the tier's fixed price today; instance pooling would reduce it, and has not been implemented. Serialisation cost for complex types at node boundaries is not measured at all.

== External validity: scale and generalisation <sec:threat-external>

*Graph scale: #ev.graphs.customer_support_nodes nodes, not hundreds.* The concrete graph has #ev.graphs.customer_support_nodes nodes and the composition graph #ev.graphs.platform_services services. Every claim about reviewability, comprehension, and the graph being "visible at a glance" is made at that scale, and graph-scale comprehension is unaddressed (@sec:open). The empirical literature lets this be stated with numbers rather than as a gesture: surveying 152 studies of graph visualisation, @yoghourdjian_exploring_2018 report roughly 120 nodes as the ceiling for completing graph-reading tasks inside two minutes, and node-link structures rated "easy" in the low hundreds against "hard" in the thousands. That is therefore deep inside the range where comprehension is not in question, and a graph of the scale this model targets sits at the edge of what has been empirically tested at all. One finding in that survey cuts the other way and is worth stating because it is testable: *density*, not node count, is what produces an unreadable result — dense graphs degrade from ten to fifteen nodes, while globally sparse ones stay legible at large counts. A capability-annotated graph with disciplined fan-in has some reason to stay sparse by construction, but that is a prediction this work does not test.

*No evidence that a graph beats text as the comprehension surface.* A stronger claim lurks near this one and should be disowned explicitly. Production systems that execute graphs at the scale in question — Dagster @dagster_2026, Airflow @airflow_2026, and alongside them Prefect, Kedro, and Bazel's dependency graphs — are without exception *authored in text*, with the graph rendered as a generated, read-only, queryable view; the one system built on genuine bidirectional graph-and-text synchronisation, Enso @enso_2026, found its fit in small-graph analyst tooling rather than in software systems. This was a survey of published documentation rather than an audit, so it is a strong indication and not a census. The premise this corpus began from concerns *authoring* by agents, on which none of that is evidence either way. But nothing found anywhere supports the adjacent proposition that a graph is a better surface than queryable text for *comprehending* a large system, and several systems built by people with every incentive to make visual authoring succeed argue against it. The inspector's scope — it views and runs, and does not author — is the position this evidence supports, and it is where the artifact already sits.

*#ev.injection.confined_count of #ev.injection.path_length path nodes on the confined tier, not the whole system.* The enforcement results of @sec:eval-tiers apply to nodes compiled to components — a majority of the demonstrated path, but not the platform graph around it, which runs on the host tier, where the same escape attempts *succeed*. A reader who takes "the demonstrator confines nodes" as a property of the system rather than of the ported nodes has the wrong picture — the two-tier arrangement is a migration path, and the host tier's gap is its cost.

*A Python host and a proof-of-concept runtime.* There is no differential evaluation, no snapshotting, no production deployment, and no concurrent execution. The runtime propagates signals along an active path in a single thread.

== Threats to the security argument itself <sec:threat-security>

These are the limits most consequential for how the paper should be read.

*No soundness result.* Nothing here connects well-typed wiring to noninterference. The validator implements a lattice; it does not prove that graphs well-typed under that lattice have the property the lattice is supposed to deliver. Every security claim in this paper is therefore a claim about *what the implemented checks reject*, not about what a sound type system would guarantee.

*Enforcement stops at the WASM boundary.* Confinement on the confined tier is unforgeable at that boundary and not at the memory level. A sandbox escape via a `wasmtime` defect is out of scope, and CHERI remains a named follow-up rather than an implemented layer.

*The free-text residual is real and is not closed by anything here.* @tab:injection reports that adversarial text survives in a permitted field on the confined tier. That row is asserted deliberately so that stronger enforcement is not misread as a stronger claim. Prompt injection is attenuated — the blast radius drops from arbitrary tool execution to a bad lookup query — and what bounds it is the capability scope, not the type and not the sandbox.

*Covert channels and distributed authority are out of scope, here and in the design — both are carried in the corpus's forward agenda @lavi_predicting_2026 rather than bounded here.* A node with a permitted capability can encode information in the timing, shape, or ordering of its legitimate outputs; a node with a network capability can in principle acquire authority out-of-band that the graph does not model. The `HTTPClient<[host, ...]>` kind makes the second concrete rather than hypothetical, and against this work rather than for it: an allowlist entry is a _host_, and a host grants every function reachable at it. Anthropic report that exact granularity failing in production — allowlisting one API host for one purpose also permitted file uploads to arbitrary accounts — and draw the conclusion this paper's framing needs, that an egress allowlist is better read as a capability grant than as a destination filter @anthropic_contain_claude_2026. A set-valued scope narrows by inclusion (@sec:validator); it does not make the thing being included any finer. Neither limit is addressed here.

== Open problems that bound these results <sec:open>

The limits above are properties of the evidence. The following are missing pieces of the design, named here because each one bounds a claim this paper makes. They are the subset of the corpus's forward agenda that this paper's results depend on; the full agenda, with its dependency ordering, belongs to the companion paper @lavi_predicting_2026.

*No soundness argument, and a lattice chosen for tractability.* The demonstrator realises a two-point lattice `Untrusted ⊑ Trusted` with discharge as the sole sanctioned upward move. The choice has more support than its simplicity suggests: W3C Trusted Types @w3c_trusted_types_2026, shipped across major browsers, is exactly this shape at scale — a sink refuses a raw string and accepts only a value minted by a registered policy, which is a two-point lattice with a single designated discharge — and the two-point-plus-discharge form is what shipped taint systems converge on rather than a richer order. What a successor must satisfy can be stated concretely, and is exactly what a locally-sound-but-non-compositional label system loses: the flow relation used by the wiring check (`required ⊑ provided`, with a node's output label a monotone function of the meet of its input labels) must be *transitive*, so a path of individually well-typed edges is itself well-typed, and *monotone*, so composing nodes cannot manufacture trust the parts did not have. Compositionality of noninterference follows from standard results #cite(<sabelfeld_language-based_2003>, supplement: [§5]), but the signal graph's wiring model must be shown to satisfy the conditions those results require — an adaptation, not a mechanical application. The two-point lattice satisfies both trivially, which is precisely why it is weak evidence for a richer one. The choice among two-point, graded (`Untrusted ⊑ Sanitised ⊑ Trusted`), and Jif-style decentralised-label @myers_decentralized_1997 designs is open.

*Contracts are checked, not verified, and the vocabulary is small on purpose.* @sec:contracts adds pre- and postconditions with blame, which closes the gap between "its tests passed" and "it satisfies its specification" — but only as far as checking goes. Contracts are evaluated on the values a run actually produces, so nothing here establishes a property over all inputs; the generative mode that would derive a property-based-test strategy from a precondition and fuzz a node body against it is designed and unbuilt. The closed vocabulary is a real limit as well as a feature: a postcondition relating output length to input length is inexpressible, and widening the language far enough to state it would require a solver and forfeit the dependency-free checker. And the deeper limit is unchanged by any of it — contracts are partial specifications, so two implementations can satisfy the same contract and still differ observably, in latency, in resource consumption, or in which variant they emit on inputs the contract does not pin down. Regeneration can change production behaviour within that slack with no check failing.

*User-level authorisation is bound but not typed.* The confused-deputy argument of @sec:security depends on capabilities being bound to an authenticated principal and propagated downstream, and @sec:identity now does that: a run binds a principal, a declared node may extend the delegation chain, and every node records both at every altitude, so the property that no node acts outside the principal bound at entry is a test rather than a sentence. What remains open is the half that would make it *static*. The scope lives on the instance and in the trace, not in the capability's type, so the validator cannot answer without executing the graph what set of principals a node could ever act as — which is the property the argument ultimately wants. The authorisation literature is clear about what the typed form costs and requires: systems that keep delegation analysable — Cedar's decidable fragment @cutler_cedar_2024, Biscuit's append-only Datalog attenuation @biscuit_2026, the policy intersection of production IAM — restrict narrowing to a monotone, enumerable operation, while those admitting unrestricted dynamism make the authority topology unknowable until run time — Zanzibar-style relationship stores @pang_zanzibar_2019 being the production instance, where the decision lives in a live external service rather than in any artifact. That has a consequence for this design sharp enough to state as a rule: a deployment that genuinely needs such a store must hold the check as a first-class capability in a `with` clause rather than calling it ambiently, or the authority decision moves somewhere no validator can see it — reintroducing, one level up, exactly the ambient authority @sec:sandbox-tier was built twice to rule out. The same trade this work already makes for trust, in other words, which is a reason to expect the design to transfer and no evidence that it does. Nor is attenuation implemented at all: a node cannot narrow the principal it was given, only inherit it.

*A merge node's ordering obligation can be classified rather than left open.* Where several upstream nodes feed one consumer, arrival order is a scheduling artifact. The durable-execution literature suggests the obligation splits cleanly: where the combinator is commutative and associative, order is unobservable and nothing need be recorded, which is the regime in which incremental evaluation @budiu_dbsp_2023 is defined at all; where it is order-sensitive, the realised choice must be journalled as data and re-injected on replay rather than re-raced. Classifying combinators on that axis is a prerequisite to both replay and differential evaluation, and it is not done here.

*Node-local state, fan-out, and multi-terminal aggregation.* The demonstrator's graphs are stateless and its runtime propagates along a single active path. Real components need session caches and rate-limit counters; real graphs need fan-out to multiple consumers and error-propagation chains. A run reaching several terminals has no single boundary value, and the runtime refuses it rather than electing a winner (@sec:subgraph) — loud, but unresolved. Whether state is an explicit feedback edge, a stateful combinator, or a capability-mediated external store is undecided, and the choice interacts with the replay and verification properties.

*Graph-scale comprehension.* The concrete graph has #ev.graphs.customer_support_nodes nodes; real systems have hundreds. Hierarchical decomposition is the expected approach, but the interaction between hierarchical abstraction, capability wiring, and trust propagation across sub-graph boundaries has not been designed, and a type error deep in a sub-graph's wiring must produce a message comprehensible at the altitude the developer is working at. The demonstrator does nothing to address this (@sec:threat-external).

*Hierarchical capability routing, and the output side of a boundary type.* When a parent provisions a capability to a sub-graph, the sub-graph must route it to the internal nodes that require it and only those. Three designs are visible: a flat parameter list matched by type with internal fan-out by convention; named capability slots bound explicitly; or structural matching on capability types. The first is settled *at the runtime* (@sec:subgraph), which is the narrowest available claim — implementable and sufficient to run the composition, not demonstrably right — and a flat list matched by type is also exactly where aliasing ambiguity lives, which is the argument for the other two. The dual question at the output side is checked but not settled: the union convention is verified against the referenced graph's terminals, while whether named output ports or an in-graph aggregation node is preferable stays open.

*CHERI, replay, and differential evaluation are untouched, and drift from the graph is detected rather than prevented.* Enforcement stops at the WASM boundary, and the trace of @sec:trace is a necessary but insufficient precursor to replay for the reasons given there. The derivation of @sec:eval-derivation is also weaker than the ecosystem already permits, which bounds the lead claim rather than the follow-ons: an over-granting component is *built and then rejected*, where composing the unwanted imports away in the WASI-Virt manner @bytecodealliance_wasi_virt_2026 would make it unbuildable. Test-then-reject is the weaker of the two — it holds only where the comparison runs — and adopting the stronger form as defence in depth on top of it is unimplemented here. Differential evaluation needs a further qualification the vision did not attach to it: the incremental-computation results it would rest on @mcsherry_differential_2013 @budiu_dbsp_2023 hold for nodes expressed as declarative operators over deltas, whereas a node that sends a reply or calls a model is not safely re-runnable on a delta without re-performing its effect. The ambition is realistic for an effect-free sub-graph and not, as stated, for the general case.

= Conclusion <sec:conclusion>

Architecture models describe; they do not constrain. This paper reports a demonstrator in which one specific constraint is mechanical: a node's declared capabilities determine the import surface of its compiled artifact, derived from the graph and checked against the built binary, so over-granting fails the build. That the components carry no WASI adapter makes the absence of filesystem, socket, environment, and clock authority a fact about the binary rather than about the host's configuration — a distinction that sounds identical in prose, names two different guarantees, and became visible only after building the tier both ways. It is the result we would keep if forced to keep one, because it is the point at which an architecture diagram stops being documentation.

Beside it, a trust lattice with no upward coercion applied uniformly to edges and node bodies rejects the laundering graph in which every edge type-checks — caught as a lattice violation rather than a type mismatch, pinned so it cannot silently become the latter. Crossings cost #d.crossing_us µs while marshalling typed values. Hierarchical composition executes, and a sub-graph cannot mint authority of its own because there is no mechanism by which it could.

The distance remaining is larger than the distance covered, and @sec:established and @sec:threats are the parts of this paper we would most want read rather than summarised again here. The short form: nothing above is a soundness result, the corpus measures implementability and not coverage, the injection finding is a demonstration and not a benchmarked rate, prompt injection is attenuated and not eliminated, and the host tier's escapes are a real gap that the two-tier arrangement makes transitional rather than acceptable. Contract _verification_, the typed form of the user-level authorisation binding, node-local state, replay, the agent tooling, and CHERI enforcement are all untouched.

What this does not settle is whether anyone can build software this way. Every node body here was written by hand, so the model's central workflow claim — that implementations are generated against typed signatures and are interchangeable artifacts — remains untested by the very artifact that makes it checkable. The machinery for testing it is now in place: if a generated body reaches for authority its `with` clause does not grant, the derivation of @sec:sandbox-tier catches it before the component ships. Whether it does, and how often, is the obvious next experiment.

